#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

/*
We model the problem over GF(2).

Grid n x m, initial light states a[i][j] in {0,1}.
Pressing a switch at (x,y) toggles every cell (i,j) with
|i-x| + |j-y| <= d.

We must determine whether all lights can be turned off.

Let variables be presses p[x][y] in GF(2).
For each cell (i,j):
sum over all (x,y) with |i-x|+|j-y|<=d of p[x][y] = a[i][j]   (mod 2)

This is a linear system. Direct Gaussian elimination is too large in general.
Key observation:
The operator is convolution with a diamond kernel over GF(2).
Using coordinates:
u = i + j
v = i - j
Then diamond becomes an axis-aligned square:
|i-x| + |j-y| <= d  <=>  max(|u-u'|, |v-v'|) <= d
(with parity constraints, but on the actual checkerboard lattice points only).

After separating the two parity classes of (u,v), the operator becomes
a 2D box filter of radius d on a rectangular grid over GF(2).

A 2D box filter with all-ones kernel factors as:
K = H * V
where H is horizontal segment length d+1? Actually on transformed lattice with step 2,
radius d becomes radius d in the compressed coordinates, and the square side is 2d+1.
Over each parity class, after compression:
output[x][y] = xor of input in rectangle [x-d..x+d] x [y-d..y+d]
Thus K = A_row * A_col, where A_row/A_col are 1D interval-xor matrices.
Hence invertibility / solvability reduces to ranks of 1D operators.

For a given parity class with dimensions R x C:
The image of K on matrices equals { A_row * X * A_col^T }.
Its rank is rank(A_row)*rank(A_col), and a matrix B is solvable iff
for every left-null vector l of A_row and every left-null vector r of A_col:
l^T B r = 0.

So we need:
1) Build the two parity-class matrices B.
2) For each dimension n and radius d, compute a basis of left-nullspace of the 1D
   interval matrix T where T[i][j]=1 iff |i-j|<=d.
   Since T is symmetric, left-nullspace = nullspace.
3) Check all bilinear constraints.

To keep it efficient, compute nullspace basis by Gaussian elimination on the band matrix.
This is okay because dimensions are at most those of transformed grids. For typical constraints
this is fine; and the problem statement provided here lacks explicit limits.

Construction of parity classes:
For original cell (i,j), let
u=i+j, v=i-j+(m-1). Then u,v in [0..n+m-2], same parity.
For parity p in {0,1}, points with u%2=p correspond to compressed coordinates:
x=(u-p)/2, y=(v-p)/2.
For fixed p, valid ranges:
x in [0..Ru-1], Ru = count of numbers in [0..U] with parity p, U=n+m-2
y in [0..Rv-1], similarly V=n+m-2
But only some pairs correspond to actual cells; in fact for rectangle grid, mapping is bijection
between cells of parity p and a skew rectangle intersection. However in compressed coordinates,
the valid set is not full rectangle.

So the simple rectangular factorization above applies if we index by original (i,j) parity classes
using:
a=(i+j-p)/2, b=(i-j+(m-1)-p)/2.
Valid points form a rectangle intersection, not full rectangle.

Alternative better approach:
Use rows elimination directly on original grid exploiting finite propagation:
Equation for row i only depends on press rows x in [i-d, i+d].
This gives a linear cellular automaton. We can enumerate first d rows of presses and propagate.
But that is exponential.

Given missing constraints, use general Gaussian elimination over bitsets, which is robust and simpler.
*/

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, m, d;
    if (!(cin >> n >> m >> d)) return 0;

    vector<string> s(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) cin >> s[i];

    int vars = n * m;
    int eqs = n * m;

    int W = (vars + 1 + 63) >> 6; // augmented matrix
    vector<vector<unsigned long long>> mat(eqs, vector<unsigned long long>(W, 0));

    auto setbit = [&](vector<unsigned long long>& row, int pos) {
        row[pos >> 6] ^= 1ULL << (pos & 63);
    };

    auto id = [&](int x, int y) {
        return x * m + y;
    };

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j < m; ++j) {
            int r = id(i, j);
            for (int x = max(0, i - d); x <= min(n - 1, i + d); ++x) {
                int rem = d - abs(i - x);
                for (int y = max(0, j - rem); y <= min(m - 1, j + rem); ++y) {
                    setbit(mat[r], id(x, y));
                }
            }
            if (s[i][j] == '1' || s[i][j] == 'X' || s[i][j] == '*')
                setbit(mat[r], vars);
        }
    }

    int row = 0;
    for (int col = 0; col < vars && row < eqs; ++col) {
        int sel = -1;
        for (int i = row; i < eqs; ++i) {
            if ((mat[i][col >> 6] >> (col & 63)) & 1ULL) {
                sel = i;
                break;
            }
        }
        if (sel == -1) continue;
        swap(mat[sel], mat[row]);

        for (int i = 0; i < eqs; ++i) {
            if (i != row && ((mat[i][col >> 6] >> (col & 63)) & 1ULL)) {
                for (int k = 0; k < W; ++k) mat[i][k] ^= mat[row][k];
            }
        }
        ++row;
    }

    bool ok = true;
    for (int i = 0; i < eqs; ++i) {
        bool all0 = true;
        for (int col = 0; col < vars; ++col) {
            if ((mat[i][col >> 6] >> (col & 63)) & 1ULL) {
                all0 = false;
                break;
            }
        }
        if (all0 && ((mat[i][vars >> 6] >> (vars & 63)) & 1ULL)) {
            ok = false;
            break;
        }
    }

    cout << (ok ? "Yes" : "No") << '\n';
    return 0;
}

